  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $(4{x}^{6}+3)\\cdot (0.33)^{2x-5}\\ge 0.$",
  "worked_solution": "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nDividing both sides of the given inequality by $(0.33)^{2x-5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{6}+3)\\cdot (0.33)^{2x-5} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(4{x}^{6}+3)\\cdot (0.33)^{2x-5}}{(0.33)^{2x-5}} & \\ge \\frac{0}{(0.33)^{2x-5}} \\\\ 4{x}^{6}+3 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, $4{x}^{6}+3\\ge 0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the solution is all real numbers.",
